content:
New documents are created at the latest model version. The mechanism, in `packages/reactor/src/client/reactor-client.ts`, is worth knowing precisely because it is a *resolution* step, not a stored default:

```ts
// latest version — resolved by reduce over registered modules
const doc = await client.createEmpty("my-org/todo");

// pinned version
const v1Doc = await client.createEmpty("my-org/todo", { documentModelVersion: 1 });
```

With no option, `createEmpty` filters registered modules by document type and reduces to the one with the highest `normalizeDocumentModelVersion(m.version)`. "Latest" therefore means *highest currently registered in this Reactor* — not highest that exists. Deploy a host whose package set lags and new documents are created at the older version, silently and without error.

With `documentModelVersion` set, resolution is an exact match and a miss is loud: `Document model not found for type: <type> with version: <n>`. Passing `0` normalizes to `1`, consistent with how legacy unstamped documents are treated.

Reads are version-agnostic. `client.find({ type: "my-org/todo" })` returns documents of every version; the Reactor resolves each document to its own module by the version stamped in its header. That is what lets v1 and v2 documents coexist in one drive with no query-side branching — and why you should never assume a document you just fetched is at the latest version.

When to pin: reproducing a bug against a specific reducer, seeding fixtures for an upgrade test, or writing a migration that must produce pre-upgrade documents on purpose.

_Source: apps/academy/docs/academy/03-Build/02-DocumentModelCreation/08-DocumentModelVersioning.md, confirmed against packages/reactor/src/client/reactor-client.ts and packages/shared/document-model/upgrades.ts (Powerhouse v6.2.2-dev.45)._
